<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erling Skjalgsson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">975–1028</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Rygekongen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erling Skjalgsson was a Norwegian political leader of the late 10th and early 11th centuries, a chieftain of Rogaland who lived on the farm Sola in Nord-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaeren">Jæren</a> and is commonly seen as the foremost defender of the traditional small kingdoms and the þing system against the Fairhair line. Baptised and married to Astrid, sister of King Olav Tryggvason, in 996, he was given the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sognefjord">Sognefjord</a> and Lindesnes to rule. He fought Olav Haraldsson at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nesjar">Battle of Nesjar</a> in 1016, and in 1022 raised a 1,000-man army to free his nephew from the king. Trapped by Olav's fleet in the Battle of Boknafjorden in 1028, he was captured and killed by Aslak Fitjaskalle.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A thrall could be freed by the owner, in the owner’s will, or by buying freedom with money earned; according to the sagas the Norwegian magnate <b>Erling Skjalgsson</b> set each of his thralls a price and let them earn it by working for themselves.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
